Test fixtures for the vamos AMS tests (docs/VAMOS_AMS_DESIGN.md §9).

netlist/  SPICE inputs and emitter goldens
vhdl/     design.vhd files captured from iverilog-sv2ghdl (WSL) for the cut tests
ams/      control files and small mixed-signal designs
spectre/  the spectre personality's fixtures (docs/VAMOS_SPECTRE_DESIGN.md §11):
          Spectre netlists and CST dumps, real PSF files, XDM decks; spectre/README
          names the origin, commit and licence of every file there

Third-party files
-----------------
The files below come from other projects and keep their own licences (see
the repository's LICENSE).  The Spectre fixtures under spectre/ bring four more
sets, each copied byte for byte at a named commit: the NetlistParse.rs Spectre
and SPICE corpus with its CST dumps (MIT, Copyright (c) JuliaHub, Inc. and
other contributors, licence text in LICENSES/MIT.txt), psf-parser's real
Spectre PSF files (MIT; its code is vendored as tests/vamos/third_party/psf_parser
with its LICENSE), psf_utils' PSF samples (GPL-3.0-or-later) and the
Xyce_Regression XDM/SPECTRE decks (GPL-3.0-or-later, Copyright 2019 NTESS).
spectre/README names each file's origin, commit and licence, and the files
left out and why.

x-heep, https://github.com/x-heep/x-heep (formerly
https://github.com/esl-epfl/x-heep): hw/ip_examples/ams/analog/adc.sp and
control.init, byte for byte.  Both came in with x-heep commit
494237ce3b792c099eb06d30182c9eeb9f19dcb6 ("integration of an AMS peripheral
example (#270)", 2023-06-14), and x-heep's main branch still had them unchanged
on 2026-10-03.  Copyright EPFL contributors; Apache License 2.0 (SPDX:
Apache-2.0), whose text is LICENSES/Apache-2.0.txt at the root of this
repository (the "see LICENSE" in their headers means x-heep's LICENSE file).
The copies:
  ams/e2e_supplies/xheep/hw/ip_examples/ams/analog/adc.sp
  ams/e2e_supplies/xheep/hw/ip_examples/ams/analog/control.init
  netlist/spice_xheep/hw/ip_examples/ams/analog/adc.sp
  ams/init_xheep_control.init                          (control.init)

PTM, the Predictive Technology Model (Nanoscale Integration and Modeling Group,
Arizona State University), http://ptm.asu.edu/: the two identical
hw/ip_examples/ams/analog/65nm_bulk.pm files, in ams/e2e_supplies/xheep/ and
netlist/spice_xheep/, are a subset of PTM's 65 nm bulk CMOS BSIM4 cards
(65nm_bulk.pm, released 22 February 2006).  x-heep's adc.sp includes that file,
and x-heep does not ship it.  The file's header says what was kept and what
differs from PTM's card.  PTM asks every user to acknowledge its URL,
http://ptm.asu.edu/, and its publications:
  W. Zhao, Y. Cao, "New generation of Predictive Technology Model for sub-45nm
  early design exploration," IEEE Transactions on Electron Devices, vol. 53,
  no. 11, pp. 2816-2823, November 2006.
  Y. Cao, T. Sato, D. Sylvester, M. Orshansky, C. Hu, "New paradigm of
  predictive MOSFET and interconnect modeling for early circuit design,"
  CICC, pp. 201-204, 2000.
